@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+# Status signals — every word is earned
+
+The owner, 2026-10-04: "the socket said, like, reconnecting or something... I thought it
+said whisper was connected... we don't want to say connected when we're not connected. We
+need exactly the right signal." This page lists every word the Dictate widget can show, and
+the one real event that is allowed to produce it. Nothing here is shown because something
+is *expected* to be true — only because it just happened.
+
+## The five stages
+
+| Stage | Word shown | The real event that earns it |
+|---|---|---|
+| **mic** | `idle` | `Dictate.render()` — nothing asked for yet |
+| | `checking the engine…` | `start()` begins — before anything is opened |
+| | `requesting…` | `detect_engine()` picked an engine; `getUserMedia()`/`SpeechRecognition.start()` about to be called |
+| | `ok` | `open_engine()`'s promise resolved — the device really opened |
+| | `refused: …` | `getUserMedia()`/`open_engine()` threw, or the up-front insecure-context/whisper-down check failed |
+| | `abort right after reload — retrying once…` | `open_engine()` caught an `AbortError` (the previous page's mic still releasing) |
+| | `timed out waiting for the device` | 8s passed in `"connecting"` — a permission prompt nobody answered |
+| **socket** (dev only) | `connected` | `Socket.singleton().connected` is `true` right now — read live, never cached |
+| | `retrying N` | the same flag is `false`; `N` is the socket's own reconnect-attempt counter (`Socket.js`'s `fails`, reset to 0 on `open()`) — never a bare "retrying" with no count |
+| | `—` | no dev socket exists (a production build, or `Socket.singleton` threw) — **never "connected"/"retrying N" as a guess** |
+| **whisper** | `checking…` / `checking again…` | `detect_engine()` is mid-request to `whisper-server`'s health check |
+| | `answering` | its `/` health check returned `r.ok` — the SERVER answered, which is all this word claims; it says nothing about any one transcription (that is `worth_sending()`/`transcribe()`'s own job, not a status word today) |
+| | `not reachable` | both health-check attempts failed |
+| | `stopped answering: …` | a live segment's `/inference` call itself failed mid-session (`close_segment()`'s catch) |
+| **session** (voice conversation) | `starting…` | `chat.js`'s `ensure()` called `Session.start()` and is still awaiting it |
+| | `started` | that promise resolved — `ctl.session` is a real id Servex gave back |
+| | `resumed` | `resume_to()` resolved — an older session's id, not a fresh one |
+| | `none` | no session has been asked for yet this page load |
+| **assistants** | `thinking…` | `Session.say()` was sent and nothing has come back yet |
+| | `answering` | `chat.js`'s `draw()` first drew a `{chat:{from:{kind:"assistant"}}}` line for this exchange, or a `stream()` token arrived |
+| | `replied` | that assistant line finished (not `fix: true`, or the stream's final token) |
+
+Every row above is read, live, off the real object that owns it — never a flag set in
+advance "because it should work by now." All five print through ONE method, `Dictate.js`'s
+own `log_step(stage, detail)`: it writes the `[dictate] stage: detail` console line AND
+redraws `$diag`, the always-visible status line under the mic — so **mic**, **socket** and
+**whisper** (there since round 2, `doc/decisions.md`) and **session**/**assistants** (added
+by this task) show up on screen in the SAME place, not split between a line the reader sees
+and a trail only the console does. `chat.js`'s own `deliver`/`draw`/stream handler call
+`panel.dictate.log_step("session"|"assistants", …)` at the real event (`Session.start()`
+going out, that promise resolving, a reply's first token, a settled reply landing) — there
+is no second, console-only path left. A real reply reads, on screen:
+
+```
+mic: idle · socket: connected · whisper: answering · session: started · assistants: replied
+```
+
+(proof: `ai/2026-10-04/dictate-live-state/shots/overview-status-line-1920.png`).
+
+## Reading the trail
+
+Every stage change prints `console.log("[dictate] <stage>: <word>")`. Loading the Dictate
+page and pressing the mic once produces, in order:
+
+```
+[dictate] socket: retrying 1          (or "connected", or "—")
+[dictate] whisper: checking…
+[dictate] whisper: answering          (or "not reachable")
+[dictate] mic: checking the engine…
+[dictate] mic: requesting…
+[dictate] mic: ok
+[dictate] session: starting…
+[dictate] session: started
+[dictate] assistants: thinking…
+[dictate] assistants: replied
+```
+
+A headless load can `page.on("console", …)` and assert this exact order — the proof for
+this task did ([`ai/2026-10-04/dictate-live-state/task.jsonl`](/framework/ai/2026-10-04/dictate-live-state/)).
+
+## Watch out
+
+- **"connected"/"retrying N" redraw the moment the real transition happens, not just on
+  the next mic press.** Round 7 found this was NOT true yet: `socket_status()` only ever
+  ran from `detect_engine()` (page load, or a press), so a socket that dropped mid-session
+  left `$diag` saying "connected" until something else happened to re-check it. Fixed by
+  giving `Socket.js` two new events, `"open"`/`"close"` (nothing listened to a connection
+  change before; `emit()`/`on()` already existed for `"data"`/`"skipped"`) — every
+  `Dictate` instance's own `watch_socket()` redraws `$diag` the instant either fires.
+- **"connected" is never shown from a cache.** `socket_status()` reads `Socket.singleton().connected`
+  fresh, every call — see `doc/decisions.md`'s note on why nothing here stores a believed state.
+- **A machine with no dev socket at all shows `—`, not a guess.** Production is static hosting
+  with nothing to connect to (`dev/Socket/Socket.js`'s own header comment) — `—` says "not
+  applicable here," which is different from "down."
+- **`session`/`assistants` are per-conversation, not per-page.** A `keep: true` mount (the ✦
+  sheet, the global session) can already be `started`/`replied` the instant a NEW mount attaches
+  to it — `chat.js`'s `attach()` redraws the whole thread at that point, same as it always did;
+  the stage words just narrate what was already happening.
